connected customer prefix (195.11.14.0/24 from router1, 200.1.1.0/24 from
router2) and installs the peer's prefix via BGP with the correct next-hop. IP
forwarding is enabled on both routers. No iptables, ip6tables, nftables, or tc
rules are present on any device that could filter or shape traffic. Interface
addresses are correct, all links are UP, and no drops or errors appear in
interface counters. Symmetric ICMP reachability (ping 0% loss in both
directions), symmetric traceroutes (3 hops each way through the expected path
pc ↔ router1 ↔ router2 ↔ pc), and successful TCP iperf3 sessions in both
directions (~143–146 Mbps, clean completion) confirm that the data plane is
fully bidirectional. The ticket symptom — replies from pc2 never reaching pc1
— does not correspond to any observable network state at the time of
investigation. The most likely explanation is a transient host-side issue
(e.g., a brief routing table flush, a crashed local process, or an application
glitch on pc1 or pc2) that self-resolved before this investigation began. No
corrective action is required on the network infrastructure.
